1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,519
Welcome back to AI Dev Tools — the Crazyrouter Podcast.

2
00:00:04,469 --> 00:00:16,167
Today we are talking about a question that sounds simple, but matters a lot in production: where does a fast model like Gemini 3.5 Flash actually fit when you compare it with Claude-style response tiers?

3
00:00:16,167 --> 00:00:19,445
A lot of teams still compare models by brand.

4
00:00:19,445 --> 00:00:21,650
Gemini versus Claude.

5
00:00:21,650 --> 00:00:24,233
OpenAI versus Anthropic.

6
00:00:24,233 --> 00:00:26,650
Google versus everyone else.

7
00:00:26,650 --> 00:00:30,707
But that is usually the wrong abstraction for engineering teams.

8
00:00:30,707 --> 00:00:35,235
In production, the better question is not: which provider is smarter?

9
00:00:35,235 --> 00:00:39,669
The better question is: which response tier should handle this task?

10
00:00:39,669 --> 00:00:41,780
Think about three tiers.

11
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:44,080
First, the fast tier.

12
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:53,926
This is for summaries, classification, extraction, support drafts, simple chat, and high-volume workflows where latency and cost matter more than deep reasoning.

13
00:00:53,926 --> 00:00:56,344
Second, the balanced tier.

14
00:00:56,344 --> 00:01:04,834
This is where you put harder writing, coding explanations, medium-complexity reasoning, and agent steps where mistakes are more expensive.

15
00:01:04,834 --> 00:01:07,382
Third, the deep reasoning tier.

16
00:01:07,382 --> 00:01:16,580
This is where you use the strongest and most expensive models for long planning, complex codebase analysis, difficult debugging, or high-risk decisions.

17
00:01:16,580 --> 00:01:24,044
Gemini 3.5 Flash is best understood as a strong fast-tier model that can sometimes reach into the lower balanced tier.

18
00:01:24,044 --> 00:01:27,747
It is not trying to replace Claude Opus-style models.

19
00:01:27,747 --> 00:01:36,415
But it can compete very seriously with Claude Haiku-style routes, and on some tasks it can even be a good first pass before escalating to Sonnet.

20
00:01:36,415 --> 00:01:47,500
We recently tested this through an OpenAI-compatible endpoint using three models: gemini-3.5-flash, claude-haiku-4-5, and claude-sonnet-4-5.

21
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:54,433
The endpoint was the China route at cn.crazyrouter.com slash v1 slash chat completions.

22
00:01:54,433 --> 00:02:05,601
The tasks were intentionally practical: strict bullet summaries, a small constraint reasoning problem, a Python bug fix, a token cost calculation, and a strict JSON output task.

23
00:02:05,601 --> 00:02:09,127
And the most useful finding was not just the leaderboard.

24
00:02:09,127 --> 00:02:11,556
It was an integration pitfall.

25
00:02:11,556 --> 00:02:21,886
When max tokens was set too low, Gemini 3.5 Flash sometimes returned HTTP 200, with finish reason set to length, but with empty content.

26
00:02:21,886 --> 00:02:27,936
That is the kind of bug that can look like success in your logs while your application gets nothing useful back.

27
00:02:27,936 --> 00:02:33,195
Once the token budget was raised to a safer value, the result changed dramatically.

28
00:02:33,195 --> 00:02:43,018
Gemini 3.5 Flash averaged about 5.65 seconds, with a median around 4.93 seconds, and passed the full small test set.

29
00:02:43,018 --> 00:02:50,554
Claude Haiku averaged around 9.13 seconds, and Claude Sonnet around 10.47 seconds in that run.

30
00:02:50,554 --> 00:02:56,568
Now, this does not mean Gemini 3.5 Flash is universally better than Claude Sonnet.

31
00:02:56,568 --> 00:02:58,985
That would be the wrong conclusion.

32
00:02:58,985 --> 00:03:07,511
Claude-style models are often stronger when you need polished writing, long-form nuance, complex coding, or careful multi-step agent planning.

33
00:03:07,511 --> 00:03:16,757
But the test does show something important: for fast, structured, API-driven work, Gemini 3.5 Flash can be a very strong first route.

34
00:03:16,757 --> 00:03:21,202
The production lesson is simple: route by task, not by brand.

35
00:03:21,202 --> 00:03:25,424
For support ticket classification, use a fast model first.

36
00:03:25,424 --> 00:03:33,219
For JSON extraction, use the model that obeys your schema most reliably, then validate output before it touches your database.

37
00:03:33,219 --> 00:03:37,594
For simple code fixes, test both fast and balanced routes.

38
00:03:37,594 --> 00:03:42,853
For complex architecture review, escalate to a stronger Claude or reasoning model.

39
00:03:42,853 --> 00:03:46,721
This is where API gateways become real infrastructure.

40
00:03:46,721 --> 00:03:55,625
If your application hard-codes one provider and one model, every pricing change, latency spike, or quality regression becomes a product problem.

41
00:03:55,625 --> 00:04:02,193
But if your application routes through one compatible API layer, model choice becomes a runtime decision.

42
00:04:02,193 --> 00:04:06,674
You can start with Gemini 3.5 Flash for low-risk tasks.

43
00:04:06,674 --> 00:04:11,261
You can fall back to Claude Haiku when latency or availability changes.

44
00:04:11,261 --> 00:04:14,634
You can escalate to Sonnet when the task gets harder.

45
00:04:14,634 --> 00:04:20,176
And you can reserve the strongest models for the small percentage of requests that actually need them.

46
00:04:20,308 --> 00:04:23,866
The metrics to watch are not only average latency.

47
00:04:23,816 --> 00:04:26,458
Watch p95 latency.

48
00:04:26,458 --> 00:04:28,486
Watch finish reason.

49
00:04:28,486 --> 00:04:30,573
Watch empty content.

50
00:04:30,573 --> 00:04:32,849
Watch JSON validity.

51
00:04:32,849 --> 00:04:34,889
Watch retry rate.

52
00:04:34,889 --> 00:04:39,075
Watch cost per successful task, not just cost per token.

53
00:04:39,075 --> 00:04:41,269
That last point matters.

54
00:04:41,269 --> 00:04:46,953
A cheap model that fails twice may be more expensive than a stronger model that succeeds once.

55
00:04:46,953 --> 00:04:51,929
A fast model that returns invalid JSON may create hidden cleanup costs.

56
00:04:51,929 --> 00:04:57,601
A slow model may be acceptable for background jobs but unacceptable for user-facing chat.

57
00:04:57,601 --> 00:05:02,106
So the real model strategy in 2026 is not picking a winner.

58
00:05:02,106 --> 00:05:04,087
It is building a ladder.

59
00:05:04,087 --> 00:05:06,068
Fast route first.

60
00:05:06,068 --> 00:05:08,156
Validate the output.

61
00:05:08,156 --> 00:05:10,750
Retry or fallback when needed.

62
00:05:10,750 --> 00:05:13,509
Escalate when the task is complex.

63
00:05:13,509 --> 00:05:15,290
Log the reason.

64
00:05:15,290 --> 00:05:17,200
Measure the outcome.

65
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:22,766
Gemini 3.5 Flash is exciting because it makes that first rung of the ladder stronger.

66
00:05:22,766 --> 00:05:26,623
Claude remains valuable because the higher rungs still matter.

67
00:05:26,623 --> 00:05:31,693
And the teams that win will not be the ones arguing about which model is best in general.

68
00:05:31,693 --> 00:05:36,564
They will be the ones measuring which model is best for each task in their own product.

69
00:05:36,564 --> 00:05:38,509
That is it for today.

70
00:05:38,509 --> 00:05:47,377
If you are building AI products, keep your model layer flexible, monitor the weird edge cases, and never trust HTTP 200 alone.

71
00:05:47,377 --> 00:05:51,611
This has been AI Dev Tools — the Crazyrouter Podcast.

72
00:05:51,611 --> 00:05:53,450
See you next time.

